[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Gameplay Tags and Hierarchical Query Systems in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gameplay Tags provide a hierarchical, dot-separated naming convention to categorize and query game state, abilities, and actor properties efficiently. Combined with Gameplay Tag Queries, they form a powerful system for decoupled game logic in Unreal Engine."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Creating and Configuring Gameplay Tags"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To start using Gameplay Tags, you must define them in your project settings. Navigate to Project Settings -> Gameplay Tags to add new tags. Use a hierarchical dot notation, such as Status.Buff.Stun or Damage.Physical.Slash."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Gameplay Tags in C++"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can expose Gameplay Tags and interact with them in C++ using the FGameplayTag and FGameplayTagContainer structures. Below is an example of adding tags to a character and checking for them."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"#include "GameplayTagContainer.h"
#include "AbilitySystemInterface.h"

// Checking for a specific tag
bool AMyCharacter::HasStunTag() const
{
    FGameplayTag StunTag = FGameplayTag::RequestGameplayTag(FName("Status.Buff.Stun"));
    return CharacterTagContainer.HasTag(StunTag);
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Utilizing Hierarchical Query Systems"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"FGameplayTagQuery allows you to evaluate complex logical conditions against a FGameplayTagContainer. Instead of writing rigid branching code, you can build dynamic queries that check for matching parent tags, exact matches, or explicit exclusions."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"bool AMyCharacter::EvaluateDamageQuery(const FGameplayTagQuery& DamageQuery, const FGameplayTagContainer& IncomingTags)
{
    return DamageQuery.Matches(IncomingTags);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Gameplay Tags"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep hierarchies shallow (maximum 3 to 4 levels deep) to maintain readability and performance."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer FGameplayTagContainer for runtime checks involving multiple states over iterating through arrays of individual tags."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Gameplay Tag Queries for reusable condition checks, such as status effect immunities or skill cost validations."}]
